the difficulty of developing with residential in this area And I just like to say that a lot of our large parcels around the community have the same issues Um if you look at Whitney Ranch um north of Del Range and west of Whitney there's a lot of land up there It's going to be hard to develop It's going to have to extend water lines It's going to have to extend a sewer line It's going to have to have lift stations It's also a large large acreage And having a data center there would be much better for our tax base than having single family homes there Um pretty much that's true anywhere in the community as far as single family homes they're usually a a drain on resources whereas a data center will be a net contributor to tax base and

but I would also like to note that um the a change in a community outlook going from a suburban sprawl type outlook to a urban go up not outlook is not a necessarily a bad thing it's just something different that Cheyenne hasn't really done So there are constrained communities that are physically constrained They're very successful I refer to both Boulder Colorado and Provo Utah Very different on the political ends of the spectrum but both very similar otherwise They have a similar amount of population They have a similar amount of constraints to growing single family homes They have a sim similar amount of incoming commuter traffic in the tens of thousands of incoming commuters to both those communities And

